@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+## Context
+
+Code under `src/domain/` must not read the clock (no `Date.now()`, no argument-less `new Date()`) and must not throw. See proposal.md.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+- Goals: correct, deterministic timestamps; testable without the real clock.
+- Non-Goals: persistence, timestamps for removal, changing ordering.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Inject the clock.** `createTaskList(now: () => string)` takes a function returning an ISO-8601 string; the domain only calls it. `createTodoList(now = () => new Date().toISOString())` in `src/index.ts` (outside the domain) supplies the real clock by default. Alternative: read the clock in the domain — forbidden.
+- **Pure task functions take the time.** `createTask(input, id, now)`, and `completeTask`, `reopenTask`, `renameTask`, `addTag`, `removeTag` get an `at` parameter that sets `updatedAt`. The list calls the clock once per operation, after validation succeeds.
+- **Failed operations do not touch `updatedAt`** (they return an error without a new task). `tag` of an already-present tag and `untag` of an absent tag still count as a change attempt and set `updatedAt`; assumption: the spec says "every change", and keeping these simple avoids special cases. Rename to the same title likewise updates.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Clock returns the same instant twice] → timestamps may be equal; acceptable.
+- [Signature changes to exported functions] → only internal to the package; `createTodoList` stays compatible.
